==English==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|meretrīx}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|meretrices}}

# A [[prostitute]] in [[Ancient Rome]].
#* {{quote-book|en|title={{w|Satyricon}}|translator={{w|W. C. Firebaugh}}|url=http://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks14/1401471h.html|year=a. 100 {{CE}}|year_published=1922|author=Petronius|authorlink=Petronius|passage=Nomus Marcellus has pointed out the difference between this class of prostitutes and the prostibula. "This is the difference between a '''meretrix''' (harlot) and a prostibula (common strumpet): a '''meretrix''' is of a more honorable station and calling; for '''meretrices''' are so named a merendo (from earning wages) because they plied their calling only by night; prostibulu because they stand before the stabulum (stall) for gain both by day and night."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Ariadne Staples|title=From Good Goddess to Vestal Virgins|year=2013|publisher=Routledge|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=cCqmzwXnHhcC&pg=PT73&dq=%22the+meretrix%22&hl=&cd=19&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22the%20meretrix%22&f=false|isbn=978-1-134-78788-3|passage=Of the two ritually important female categories, ''matrona'' and '''''meretrix''''', it was the ''matrona'' that was held at a strict ritual distance. {{...}} The domain of the '''''meretrix''''' was not held at a ritual distance. The boundary between male and female was not quite so stark when the female belonged to  the category of prostitute.}}

